# The shadow-oracle ENGINE this checkout is judged by (Phase C cutover).
#
# The money oracle is now the Rust `busbar-oracle` crate in GetBusbar/busbar-release. bin/oracle
# obtains that binary from the SOURCE pin below and runs it against this directory's DATA. This
# source pin (repo + git ref + crate) REPLACES the old archive digest-pin in oracle.pin: the thing
# that must not change quietly is which judge ran, and a git ref + `cargo build --locked` from that
# tree is the reproducible source identity (the Cargo.lock at the ref pins every dependency). The
# ref moves only by editing this file, exactly as the old sha256 did.
#
# oracle.pin (the Python archive digest-pin) is KEPT for this pass as a fallback and is NOT deleted;
# the Python tool is retired in a later step (see the Phase C cutover checklist).
#
# 2026-09-22 (DECISION #80): moved to the commit that ports the corpus GENERATOR into the judge, so
# `./bin/oracle cells --check` regenerates the 2318-cell corpus and refuses byte drift instead of
# only validating the committed file against itself. busbar's own generator
# (`testing/shadow-oracle/cells/__init__.py`) is deleted in the same commit as this bump — a judge
# older than this ref cannot check the corpus it is asked to judge, so the two move together.
# NOTE: this ref is a feature branch head (`port/oracle-cells-generate`), which is the same shape
# the previous pin had; re-pin to the integration/merge commit when that branch lands.
#
# 2026-09-22: moved to the commit that makes `--filter` real on the paths that took it and ignored
# it. `HarnessArgs::into_config()` discarded `--filter` (so `replay --filter 'zzz-no-such-cell'`
# printed the same 913 DIVERGED rows as an unfiltered replay), and the record driver put `--family`
# in `record.sh`'s `--filter` slot while never passing the id regex at all. Selection itself was
# never broken: `select_cells` is byte-identical to the refs the bug was filed against, and over the
# committed corpus `^billing\|` selects 14, `ledger` 6, `^(billing|ledger|config)\|` 20. See
# findings-2026-09-22-record-filter.md for the measurements and what actually caused the original
# "0 cells" (a fixed port band shared by three concurrent runs, fixed earlier by 00759df).
# NOTE: still a feature branch head (`fix/oracle-recorder-residual-gaps`), the same shape as the
# previous two pins; re-pin to the integration/merge commit when that branch lands.
#
# 2026-09-22: moved to the commit that lands the three oracle-baseline owner rulings, which together
# change what a GREEN oracle run is allowed to claim. (1) The clock fields stop normalising: `as_of`,
# `window.start` and `window.end` flattened to `0` before comparison, so a response carrying a wrong
# rate-card epoch over a wrong billing window was byte-identical to a right one — measured with the
# shipped binary on the shape of `ledger|rate-history|as-of`, spend figure identical to the byte. A
# cell may now declare `"clock": {...}`; the recorder binds it into the request as `{CLOCK:<path>}`
# (the seam that already binds minted key ids and listener addrs) so busbar is DRIVEN to the instant
# and the echo is compared EXACTLY, with a fixed `<CLOCK-PIN-MISS:...>` marker keeping the red
# deterministic. `latency_ms` stays masked and is refused as a pin. An UNPINNED cell normalizes
# byte-identically to before, so no golden moves and none is re-recorded here. (2) A cell with no
# prior golden reports UNBASELINED, never PASS: measured over this corpus, 1402 of 2318 cells used to
# produce no ledger row at all and could not be told from a success; they now carry their own row,
# `unbaselined.txt`, `totals.unbaselined` and a summary line, and reach the turnstile as NoGolden.
# It does NOT set the exit code — making gaps red is a separate ruling. (3) The golden is a dated
# history: `--baseline-version <V>` resolves `golden/<V>` and REFUSES a version that does not exist,
# naming it and listing what the history holds, instead of silently falling back; `golden_version`
# is no longer the literal "1.5.5", so `golden/1.6.0` can land at the cut and 1.6.1 be judged
# against it. `--golden-dir` still works and reads the version off the recording's own meta.json.
# NOTE: still a feature branch head (`fix/oracle-recorder-residual-gaps`), the same shape as the
# previous three pins; re-pin to the integration/merge commit when that branch lands.
#
# To move the pin: set release_ref to the new busbar-release commit and re-run.
release_repo=https://github.com/GetBusbar/busbar-release.git
release_ref=7e00b54
crate=busbar-release-oracle
bin=busbar-oracle
